# **III. The Friendship**

## **Structure**

This is a long-term male friendship, same age, rooted in shared cultural language (internet subcultures, drugs, music), maintained primarily through text with intermittent in-person visits that appear to be significant events for both parties.

The asymmetry that matters: Dan talks, Tom reacts. Dan evangelizes, Tom evaluates and occasionally converts (from ChatGPT skeptic to ChatGPT relationship therapist in six weeks). Dan is emotionally the louder party; Tom is practically the more resourced one. They need each other for different things.

## **The NYC Visit**

The November-December 2024 data captures Tom visiting Dan in New York City — apparently a significant trip for Tom, who navigated the subway largely alone, saw Enter Shikari, kept the concert poster (now framed), and was impressed enough with himself for making the Amtrak back alone that Dan acknowledges it as a genuine accomplishment. Dan saved Tom the Enter Shikari poster. Tom had it framed.

This is the kind of thing that doesn’t get said directly. The poster is the statement.

## **How They Fight**

They don’t, really. The closest thing to conflict in the dataset is the January 2025 sub incident where Dan accuses Tom of taking his last piece (Tom didn’t), and the earlier reference to Dan getting Tom “banned from Xanax” — which Tom apparently found funny in retrospect. There is no record of a real argument. They agree to disagree on Schranz vs DnB, on Will Stancil, on whether Cobbler is based or reddit. These are maintained as ongoing positions rather than resolved disputes.

## **What They Say That They Don’t Say**

Dan, signing off from a crisis conversation: “Thanks for the shoulder to sob on. Don’t ask me to do the same you bitch loser piece of shit I don’t wanna hear your fucking stupid problems.” Then: “Love you buddy.”

Tom, when Dan says he’s coming back to Pittsburgh: “I’m not mad about it. I mean, sucks that you have to come back. I feel for you. Like. Sincerely. I do.”

These are love letters. Neither of them would use that word.

## **The Arc (Both Datasets Combined)**

November 2024: Dan is in NYC, in a relationship crisis with Annie that he hasn’t yet named as a crisis. Tom visits. They see a show. They do drugs. It seems fine.

February 2025: Annie’s episode. Dan in the dark, no subs, her parents in the city, “magnum opus spiral.” He texts Tom at 4am. Tom offers to supply everything he needs when he gets back to Pittsburgh. Dan is moving back.

January 2026: Both of them are back in their respective Pittsburgh-area lives. Tom is falling in love. Dan is making music and going deep on AI. The conversations are long and fast and funny. The crisis energy of the winter has been replaced by something more stable.

March 2026: Tom’s relationship is imploding. Dan starts a job for the first time in a long time. Dan uses Claude to analyze 130,000 messages of his relationship. Tom uses ChatGPT to not lose his girlfriend. Dan texts Tom at 5:44am on a work morning just to talk.

The final message in the dataset is Tom saying: “My AI thinks it’s my dad.” It is, functionally, a pretty good summary.

